<!-- Website teaching layer; source remains unchanged. Primary-source review: batch-2.json. -->
<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A commuter sends a message just before the train loses signal. The app shows it as queued. After reconnecting, the server stores it, but the reply never reaches the phone. Should the phone delete it, resend it, or show failure?</p>

Separate acceptance, execution and acknowledgment. A persisted row answers whether the phone remembers pending intent. It does not answer whether the server already performed that operation.

<!-- @before Internal Mechanics :: guide -->
### Choose the operation's identity before its retry policy

Keep one stable operation ID across attempts. A worker claims pending work, performs it under the server's deduplication contract, and acknowledges only a confirmed outcome. A timeout can mean the request never arrived, the server is still working, or the reply was lost. Blindly assigning a new ID on each attempt can turn one user action into multiple mutations.

[Source: durable intent, duplicate messages and idempotent consumers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/cloud-design-patterns/transactional-outbox.html).

